WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

1
00:00:00.310 --> 00:00:03.380
Finding the beauty and two opposing views.

2
00:00:03.380 --> 00:00:05.010
Well, I like to call that

3
00:00:05.010 --> 00:00:08.210
the pretty little middle AKA balance.

4
00:00:08.210 --> 00:00:10.510
Balance is where two halves make a hole

5
00:00:10.510 --> 00:00:14.620
and one area this is particularly important; parenting.

6
00:00:14.620 --> 00:00:16.560
Helping us today is my good friend,

7
00:00:16.560 --> 00:00:18.970
friend of the show and parenting educator,

8
00:00:18.970 --> 00:00:20.630
Dr. Aliza Pressman.

9
00:00:20.630 --> 00:00:22.340
She's the co founding director

10
00:00:22.340 --> 00:00:24.480
of the Mount Sinai Parenting Center.

11
00:00:24.480 --> 00:00:27.110
And she has a podcast called Raising Good Humans.

12
00:00:27.110 --> 00:00:30.330
She's one prestigious lady, Dr. Aliza,

13
00:00:30.330 --> 00:00:31.893
thank you for being here.

14
00:00:31.893 --> 00:00:33.560
(audience clapping)
Hello.

15
00:00:33.560 --> 00:00:36.670
Let me just come out of the gate and ask you

16
00:00:36.670 --> 00:00:39.150
what are some of the main issues

17
00:00:39.150 --> 00:00:41.570
that parents can't seem to agree on?

18
00:00:41.570 --> 00:00:44.570
Anything that pulls up your childhood memory

19
00:00:44.570 --> 00:00:47.120 line:15%
of being parented, which is basically discipline

20
00:00:47.120 --> 00:00:50.280 line:15%
your parenting styles are reflected in discipline.

21
00:00:50.280 --> 00:00:54.550
Things related to boundaries setting.

22
00:00:54.550 --> 00:00:56.280
I know a couple going through it,

23
00:00:56.280 --> 00:00:59.760
Rachel and Brad are happily married parents of three

24
00:00:59.760 --> 00:01:02.590
but here's a look at what's happening in their house.

25
00:01:02.590 --> 00:01:04.636
Drew. This is a perfect example.

26
00:01:04.636 --> 00:01:06.460
(gentle music)

27
00:01:06.460 --> 00:01:09.810 line:15%
I literally just made the kids as a real fruit smoothie

28
00:01:09.810 --> 00:01:11.250 line:15%
and then Brad gets home from work

29
00:01:11.250 --> 00:01:13.560 line:15%
and he's giving them packaged fruit snacks.

30
00:01:13.560 --> 00:01:15.841
A fruit snack for you.

31
00:01:15.841 --> 00:01:16.932
(indistinct)

32
00:01:16.932 --> 00:01:18.492
And here's one fruit snack for you.

33
00:01:18.492 --> 00:01:19.325
(baby babbling)

34
00:01:19.325 --> 00:01:21.310
That kind of stuff drives me crazy.

35
00:01:21.310 --> 00:01:25.010 line:15%
I want to make sure that the twins don't get hungry

36
00:01:25.010 --> 00:01:27.830 line:15%
because if they do then it's meltdown city.

37
00:01:27.830 --> 00:01:30.720
Sometimes it takes a while to cut up fresh fruit

38
00:01:32.410 --> 00:01:34.813
but you're just not sure that they're gonna eat them.

39
00:01:36.090 --> 00:01:38.360 line:15%
Nine 9 times out of 10, when I get home from work

40
00:01:38.360 --> 00:01:41.320 line:15%
and Brad's already with the kids, they're on their tablets.

41
00:01:41.320 --> 00:01:42.333
So let's just see!

42
00:01:42.333 --> 00:01:45.073
(hinges squeaking)

43
00:01:45.073 --> 00:01:47.153
Hi, guys.
Welcome home.

44
00:01:47.153 --> 00:01:49.023
Hey, what are you doing June Brad.

45
00:01:50.941 --> 00:01:53.540
I'm watching my tablet.

46
00:01:53.540 --> 00:01:54.490
It's educational.

47
00:01:55.500 --> 00:01:57.230 line:15%
Sometimes I need to get things done.

48
00:01:57.230 --> 00:02:00.742 line:15%
I'm not bad at using screen time to occupy the children.

49
00:02:00.742 --> 00:02:02.380
(gentle music)

50
00:02:02.380 --> 00:02:05.110
It is very frustrating for me when

51
00:02:05.110 --> 00:02:07.680 line:15%
I'm the only one that's trying to make an effort

52
00:02:07.680 --> 00:02:12.680 line:15%
to make dinner time, a nice family conversation.

53
00:02:13.193 --> 00:02:14.982
(child yelling)

54
00:02:14.982 --> 00:02:16.822
(child groans)

55
00:02:16.822 --> 00:02:18.590
(child yelling)

56
00:02:18.590 --> 00:02:21.436
When it's time to sit down for dinner, I'm just tired.

57
00:02:21.436 --> 00:02:23.610
(child yelling)

58
00:02:23.610 --> 00:02:26.220
All day, I'm telling them what they can, what they can't do.

59
00:02:26.220 --> 00:02:29.030 line:15%
I just want to sit down, have a nice meal with my wife

60
00:02:29.030 --> 00:02:31.490 line:15%
and not have to yell at the kids. (chuckles)

61
00:02:31.490 --> 00:02:32.510 line:15%
And when I say yell,

62
00:02:32.510 --> 00:02:36.369
not have to tell the kids what they can and they can't do.

63
00:02:36.369 --> 00:02:38.790
(child yelling)

64
00:02:38.790 --> 00:02:39.987
Every night.

65
00:02:39.987 --> 00:02:40.839
(kid groans)

66
00:02:40.839 --> 00:02:41.913
(audience clapping)

67
00:02:41.913 --> 00:02:44.950
Brad and Rachel, thank you for joining us

68
00:02:44.950 --> 00:02:49.090
from Lake Ozark, Missouri and being here with us.

69
00:02:49.090 --> 00:02:51.287
Thanks for coming on this show.

70
00:02:51.287 --> 00:02:52.450
Thank you for having us.
Thank you for having us.

71
00:02:52.450 --> 00:02:54.350
Yeah, we need some help.

72
00:02:54.350 --> 00:02:56.080
Rachel, do you feel really

73
00:02:56.080 --> 00:02:58.970
like the kids see you as the bad cop?

74
00:02:58.970 --> 00:02:59.973
Oh, for sure.

75
00:03:00.980 --> 00:03:03.090
And I try, I feel like I'm with the kids

76
00:03:03.090 --> 00:03:05.470
by myself more often than he is.

77
00:03:05.470 --> 00:03:07.330
When he's home, we're both around,

78
00:03:07.330 --> 00:03:10.860
so I feel like what I do

79
00:03:10.860 --> 00:03:12.950
there's just more pressure on what I do.

80
00:03:12.950 --> 00:03:15.640
And I feel like since I've been the disciplinary all day

81
00:03:15.640 --> 00:03:18.520
I'll wait for him to sort of step in when he gets home

82
00:03:18.520 --> 00:03:20.350
and do some disciplining.

83
00:03:20.350 --> 00:03:22.970
And it just seems like it doesn't necessarily happen.

84
00:03:22.970 --> 00:03:26.430
So to the kids, it's just like I'm the disciplinarian

85
00:03:26.430 --> 00:03:27.670
and he's the fun one.

86
00:03:27.670 --> 00:03:29.497
Well, and then from my perspective, you know,

87
00:03:29.497 --> 00:03:31.200
I have a full-time job.

88
00:03:31.200 --> 00:03:32.780
I'm also in the Navy reserves.

89
00:03:32.780 --> 00:03:35.430
So I'm gone one weekend, a month, two weeks a year.

90
00:03:35.430 --> 00:03:37.330
So I'm just not home a lot.

91
00:03:37.330 --> 00:03:39.320
And so, you know, I don't want to come home

92
00:03:39.320 --> 00:03:42.544
and then just be the disciplinary for the children,

93
00:03:42.544 --> 00:03:46.130
you know, I want them to like me, of course,

94
00:03:46.130 --> 00:03:47.840
'cause I'm not around as much.

95
00:03:47.840 --> 00:03:50.420
Aliza, let me ask you a question.

96
00:03:50.420 --> 00:03:54.040
Do you think kids see them that black and white

97
00:03:54.040 --> 00:03:55.840
as good cop, bad cop?

98
00:03:55.840 --> 00:03:58.290
I love that question because what Brad just said

99
00:03:58.290 --> 00:04:00.740
when he said, "I want them to like me",

100
00:04:00.740 --> 00:04:03.850
there's an assumption that they're not going to like you

101
00:04:03.850 --> 00:04:05.440
because you give them boundaries

102
00:04:05.440 --> 00:04:08.110
or have some expectation of their behavior,

103
00:04:08.110 --> 00:04:10.200
but we don't actually know

104
00:04:10.200 --> 00:04:11.900
what another human being is thinking

105
00:04:11.900 --> 00:04:14.520
and so it's a really good opportunity to say,

106
00:04:14.520 --> 00:04:18.520
maybe they feel really safe because there are boundaries

107
00:04:18.520 --> 00:04:23.520
and there's some sense of what is expected of them

108
00:04:23.550 --> 00:04:26.490 line:15%
but with sensitivity and kindness and a sense of humor.

109
00:04:26.490 --> 00:04:28.720 line:15%
So there's a way to do both of those things

110
00:04:28.720 --> 00:04:30.380
and be liked by your kids.

111
00:04:30.380 --> 00:04:33.300
You're still their guide, not their buddy.

112
00:04:33.300 --> 00:04:35.270
And so you can have both of those

113
00:04:35.270 --> 00:04:36.550
but it makes so much sense

114
00:04:36.550 --> 00:04:38.880
if you feel like, well, I'm not there very much.

115
00:04:38.880 --> 00:04:40.310
I want them to like me.

116
00:04:40.310 --> 00:04:42.670
So I just want to also straighten that out,

117
00:04:42.670 --> 00:04:46.840
your kids will have respect for you and feel safe

118
00:04:46.840 --> 00:04:49.830
when you create certain predictable boundaries

119
00:04:49.830 --> 00:04:51.910
but also have time for fun.

120
00:04:51.910 --> 00:04:54.310
And you don't need to be so attached

121
00:04:54.310 --> 00:04:55.680
to every one of those rules.

122
00:04:55.680 --> 00:04:59.800
So on the other side of it, letting go and saying

123
00:04:59.800 --> 00:05:00.930
the world is not going to end

124
00:05:00.930 --> 00:05:03.360
if we have a little bit of a crazy night.

125
00:05:03.360 --> 00:05:05.830
It might be that you still go to bed on time

126
00:05:05.830 --> 00:05:09.280
or you do whatever it is that's like the bigger picture

127
00:05:09.280 --> 00:05:10.300
of the boundaries

128
00:05:10.300 --> 00:05:12.920
but the little details don't matter so much.

129
00:05:12.920 --> 00:05:16.880
Already your tone with each other, isn't negative.

130
00:05:16.880 --> 00:05:19.220
You can really provoke an emotion

131
00:05:19.220 --> 00:05:22.490
with a certain tone of the way you're saying things.

132
00:05:22.490 --> 00:05:26.220
And you guys seem like a really strong, happy couple.

133
00:05:26.220 --> 00:05:27.550
And I think what you're going through

134
00:05:27.550 --> 00:05:30.730
is just really typical and we get it.

135
00:05:30.730 --> 00:05:35.730
Yeah, we really try to actively put our marriage first

136
00:05:36.210 --> 00:05:39.400
and be parents second, even though it's not really

137
00:05:40.820 --> 00:05:41.653
I don't know, it just-

138
00:05:41.653 --> 00:05:43.210
It's not easy to do at this stage.

139
00:05:43.210 --> 00:05:44.640
You know, 'cause our twins are two and a half

140
00:05:44.640 --> 00:05:46.640
and we have a 10 month old daughter.

141
00:05:46.640 --> 00:05:49.190
So it's not easy to put each other first

142
00:05:49.190 --> 00:05:51.830
and then take care of the children.

143
00:05:51.830 --> 00:05:56.460
Well, Aliza, you used a certain word and it was shaming.

144
00:05:56.460 --> 00:06:00.250
When you make people feel ashamed for the approach

145
00:06:00.250 --> 00:06:03.210
of sort of trying to figure out your differences.

146
00:06:03.210 --> 00:06:04.700
Please talk to that.

147
00:06:04.700 --> 00:06:09.000 line:15%
Yeah, when you jump into criticizing each other

148
00:06:09.000 --> 00:06:12.320 line:15%
and shamming each other about your parenting style,

149
00:06:12.320 --> 00:06:13.980
all that really does is take us

150
00:06:13.980 --> 00:06:15.760
into a fight-flight-or-freeze mode.

151
00:06:15.760 --> 00:06:18.480
That's just a stress response that anybody gets.

152
00:06:18.480 --> 00:06:20.610
So what's great is

153
00:06:20.610 --> 00:06:22.570
if you are putting your marriage first,

154
00:06:22.570 --> 00:06:26.200
you're removing or at least can try to remove

155
00:06:26.200 --> 00:06:27.770
the judgment, the shame.

156
00:06:27.770 --> 00:06:29.880
So it's not about I'm doing this right

157
00:06:29.880 --> 00:06:31.360
you're doing this wrong.

158
00:06:31.360 --> 00:06:33.350
It's about, let's figure out

159
00:06:33.350 --> 00:06:36.070
how to do this in a way that is comfortable for both of us

160
00:06:36.070 --> 00:06:39.220
since we come from totally different approaches.

161
00:06:39.220 --> 00:06:42.440
Also, you guys said that every time you come home,

162
00:06:42.440 --> 00:06:45.060
I heard sort of like there's a setup,

163
00:06:45.060 --> 00:06:47.870
there's a dynamic where you're expecting it

164
00:06:47.870 --> 00:06:50.720
to go a certain way and inevitably does

165
00:06:50.720 --> 00:06:52.200
and that might be a let down.

166
00:06:52.200 --> 00:06:55.200
And I think what I'd love to figure out

167
00:06:55.200 --> 00:06:57.001
is how we change that.

168
00:06:57.001 --> 00:06:59.584 line:15%
(upbeat music)

